<h2 id="sec-2">How We Tested</h2>
<p>Our testing protocol involved real yoga sessions across multiple styles — Hatha, Vinyasa, and Yin — to evaluate each mat under authentic conditions. We assessed:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dry Grip:</strong> How well does the mat perform in standard practice conditions?</li>
<li><strong>Wet Grip:</strong> Essential for hot yoga — does it slip when sweaty?</li>
<li><strong>Cushioning:</strong> Is there enough padding for knees and joints without being unstable?</li>
<li><strong>Rollability:</strong> Does it lay flat after unrolling, or does it curl persistently?</li>
<li><strong>Odor:</strong> Does it have a strong chemical smell out of the box?</li>
<li><strong>Portability:</strong> Weight and whether it rolls compactly for transport</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="sec-3">Top Picks Reviewed</h2>

<h3>1. Manduka PRO Lite — Best Overall</h3>
<p>The Manduka PRO Lite is the lightest variant of Manduka's legendary PRO line, weighing just 1.5 kg while maintaining the brand's renowned 4.7mm cushioning. The closed-cell surface prevents sweat and bacteria from absorbing into the mat, making it hygienic and long-lasting. After six months of daily use, it showed virtually no wear.</p>
<p>The lifetime guarantee is unmatched in the industry — if your mat develops a defect, Manduka will replace it. This makes the $110 price tag an investment rather than an expense.</p>
<div style="background:#F7F4EF;border-left:3px solid #C9A962;padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0">
<p style="margin:0;font-size:0.95rem"><strong>Best for:</strong> Serious practitioners who want a mat that lasts a decade or more.</p>
